  # ErrHandleLost rather than with a lookup miss, because any-error-will-do would have called a plane that
  # lost the handle a proof about signalling. The sixth task of a run is REFUSED at the shipped default of
  # five — counted from the DATABASE and from the process table, never from a number we returned — and the
  # machine ceiling is counted ACROSS TENANTS, or every tenant would get twenty and the Mac would get as
  # many as there are tenants. And a settled task's log is deleted past PALAI_BACKGROUND_LOG_TTL while a
  # STILL RUNNING task's is not, which is the half that makes the sweep safe rather than merely tidy. And the
  # tenth is the kill path's own asymmetry, found by review rather than by a RED: killing a task that had
  # ALREADY finished stays idempotent but claims nothing, because recording `killed` for a build that
  # exited on its own would both lie in the audit record and settle the row before the sweep could read
  # the exit code the model asked about.
  #
  # THE EIGHT E26 T3 NAMES ARE SPELLED OUT ONE BY ONE for the same reason, and the reason has now been
  # rediscovered by three consecutive tasks: not one word already on this line is a substring of any of
  # them. They are THE PARK, driven through the WHOLE of ExecuteAttempt — a scripted engine spawns a real
  # detached process by tool name and then reports run.terminal{completed}, and the run must reach
  # `waiting` with ExecuteAttempt returning NIL, because a park that returns an error is a failed attempt
  # the ladder retries. Four of the eight are the negatives without which the park is free: a run with
  # nothing running still COMPLETES; a `failed` terminal does not park; a handle that probes `lost` or
  # cannot be probed at all does not park either, because a run waiting on a process nothing may watch is
  # a run only a reaper frees. Two are §3.6 D3 — the response reads `waiting_for_tool` during the park and
  # `in_progress` before it, which is ResponseTable's first production use in this tree's history. One is
  # monotonicity: waiting_for_tool never lands on a terminal response. One reads the real session journal
  # and refuses any run terminal in it, which is what keeps every attached SSE consumer connected across
  # the park (the endpoint's own half is untagged, beside the handler).
  # THE SIX TOOL-ERROR NAMES ARE SPELLED OUT ONE BY ONE, and this is now the SEVENTH consecutive task to
  # write that sentence: not one word already on this line is a substring of any of them. They are THE
  # WEDGE — reproduced live on 2026-08-01, where `file read "README"` (missing) and
  # `file read "../../../../etc/passwd"` (correctly REFUSED) each left the run `running` forever while
  # `shell false` (exit code 1) completed cleanly. What they drive is the REAL dispatchTool over a REAL
  # spine with the REAL FileTool against a REAL directory: a missing file is ANSWERED to the model, its
  # row reaches `completed` rather than the `executing` the reconciler escalates to manual_resolution,
  # and the same run then reads the file that IS there. Two of the six are the halves without which the
  # other four are free: an UNCLASSIFIED error must still abort the attempt and leave its row for the
  # reconciler (or the fix has become "every error is a result", which swallows a fence violation), and
  # a REFUSED TRAVERSAL must still refuse — asserted against an out-of-workspace canary file whose bytes
  # must appear in neither the delivered result nor the committed row, with the leak assertion ordered
  # BEFORE the code assertion so a weakened containment check reports as a leak rather than as a naming
  # problem. The last three are the BOUND the fix had to bring with it, because delivering the error
  # removes the accidental stop the wedge was: sixteen refusals per attempt, `0` unbounded and written
  # explicitly, and a REPLAYED refusal counted like a fresh one so a crash loop cannot reset it.
  # THE FOUR E26 T6 NAMES ARE SPELLED OUT ONE BY ONE, and this is the SIXTH consecutive task to write
  # that sentence: not one word already on this line is a substring of any of them. They are THE
  # CREDENTIAL, and the epic's sharpest question (§0.4) — a background task DOES carry an environment
  # value, so the value's lifetime stopped being the Execute call and became the TASK. What they run
  # against a REAL Postgres, a REAL detached process and the REAL AES-256-GCM secret store: a build that
  # prints its own environment is masked at BOTH places those bytes reach a model — the shipped
  # palai.workspace.file read AND the shipped exit notice's 2 KiB excerpt — from ONE function, asserted in
  # one test because two redaction points would be two chances to diverge, with the raw file on disk
  # asserted to still contain the value so a passing read is a read that masked something. No field of
  # background_tasks, events, commands or tool_calls carries a value, measured by DECODING every column
  # the database returns and re-walking anything that parses as JSON or base64 — never a raw byte scan,
  # which over an encoded field can never fail and which this repository shipped once. A task whose run
  # carries a credential is REFUSED under PALAI_BACKGROUND_MAX_WALL_TIME=0, with the negative half in the
  # same function (the identical call with no environment is accepted and records a NULL deadline),
